WEBVTT

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.610
We've added in
controls for our player, so

00:00:07.622 --> 00:00:11.760
then that way we can
move them up if we press

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.239
the up key or backwards
if we press the down key.

00:00:16.465 --> 00:00:23.520
If we press the left or right arrow keys,
you can see that we rotate around our scene.

00:00:23.521 --> 00:00:28.080
So now what we want to do is we actually
want to push the player in the direction that

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.050
the camera's facing,
so for example, if we're

00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:39.280
facing this way, we want our player
to go directly up in our game view.

00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:50.160
Now an interesting to note is this
button in the top left for global or local.

00:00:53.039 --> 00:01:01.040
You can see when I change it, the
positions of those arrows actually change.

00:01:01.041 --> 00:01:04.728
So currently you can
see actually what is

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.800
the y direction of our
ball as it's rotating.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:14.800
If we take a look at our focal point, we
can actually see which way is currently the

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:20.080
front of our focal point, the
direction that our camera's facing.

00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:23.966
So if we rotate our
camera and we watch these

00:01:23.978 --> 00:01:27.760
axes, you can actually
see the z axis pointing

00:01:27.761 --> 00:01:30.400
in the direction that
our camera is pointing.

00:01:31.599 --> 00:01:35.520
So you can see our camera,
there's the direction in our game.

00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:39.124
As we rotate, you can
actually see that the

00:01:39.136 --> 00:01:42.760
camera is following
those local coordinates.

00:01:42.761 --> 00:01:47.920
Now it's helpful to use local
coordinates in a case like this.

00:01:47.921 --> 00:01:49.416
When you're actually
trying to set up your

00:01:49.428 --> 00:01:51.080
scene and set up your
environment and you want

00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:53.979
everything to be in the
same place, using something

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.560
like our global coordinate
system is a lot better.

00:01:57.561 --> 00:02:01.600
So then that way you can make sure that
when you're changing the positions of things

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:03.718
or you're changing
the rotation or anything

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.960
like that, you can always
make sure that when

00:02:05.961 --> 00:02:08.120
you drag these arrows,
it doesn't drag it off.

00:02:08.121 --> 00:02:14.640
For example, if I change this to local and
I rotate the y, when I drag the z, it's not

00:02:14.641 --> 00:02:19.480
actually going directly forward
on the z, it's going at an angle.

00:02:19.481 --> 00:02:21.480
So we'll reset all of that.

00:02:21.481 --> 00:02:26.440
So using global and using local
are helpful for different situations.

00:02:26.441 --> 00:02:31.362
So in our player controller
class, what we're actually

00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:36.680
going to do is get a
reference to our focal point.

00:02:36.681 --> 00:02:41.560
So that way we can know when the focal point
is facing in a certain direction, we will

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.400
move that player in
the direction it's facing.

00:02:44.401 --> 00:02:50.880
So we'll create a new private
since our focal point is a game object.

00:02:50.881 --> 00:02:56.360
We need a game object variable
and we'll just call this the focal point.

00:02:56.361 --> 00:02:58.320
So we avoid confusion.

00:02:58.321 --> 00:03:03.600
In our start method, we want to
get a reference to that focal point.

00:03:03.601 --> 00:03:06.800
So we'll get our focal point
variable and we'll set it.

00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:10.369
Now in this case,
because our game object is

00:03:10.381 --> 00:03:13.800
in the scene, we can
use game object.find.

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:17.800
So this will find that game
object in the project hierarchy.

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:19.360
And we can actually just use the name.

00:03:19.361 --> 00:03:25.880
So we called it focal point
with capital P and F and spaces.

00:03:25.881 --> 00:03:28.400
So now we have a
reference to that focal point.

00:03:28.401 --> 00:03:30.851
So now instead of
moving the player in the

00:03:30.863 --> 00:03:33.560
forward direction of
our world, we'll actually

00:03:33.561 --> 00:03:39.280
make it the forward direction
that our camera is pointing in.

00:03:39.281 --> 00:03:46.040
So if we use our focal point and we get its
current transform and we get the forward

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:56.480
direction that it's facing, now if we go
into Unity, when we press play on the editor,

00:03:56.481 --> 00:03:59.280
you can see that I can still
move forward or backwards.

00:03:59.281 --> 00:04:01.930
But then if I rotate the
camera, say 90 degrees,

00:04:01.942 --> 00:04:05.160
now when I move forward,
it goes in that direction.

00:04:05.161 --> 00:04:07.880
And when I go backwards, it
goes in the opposite direction.

00:04:07.881 --> 00:04:11.289
So now we can actually
use our camera to be

00:04:11.301 --> 00:04:15.120
able to control how our
ball moves in the scene.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.902
So what you're going
to do is you're going

00:04:17.914 --> 00:04:22.840
to add your own private game
object variable for the focal point.

00:04:22.841 --> 00:04:25.968
When you're start
method, you're going to get

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.120
a reference to the
focal point in our project

00:04:29.121 --> 00:04:32.840
hierarchy and set that
to our own variable.

00:04:32.841 --> 00:04:35.698
And then in our update
method, instead of

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.000
using the vector 3 dot
forward, you're actually

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.840
going to use the forward
direction of our focal point.

00:04:44.841 --> 00:04:45.841
Now it's your turn.